.TH NEDMAIL 1
.SH NAME
nedmail \- reading mail
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B upas/nedmail
[
.B -f
.I mailbox
] [
.B -m
.I name
] [
.B -c
.I smtpconfig
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nedmail
is an interactive command-line mail reader, after Plan 9's
.IR nedmail .
It is a client of
.IR upas/fs (1):
it lists the numbered message directories a mailbox presents under
.BR /mail/fs ,
reads each message's parsed
.B info
and its content files, and deletes through
.BR /mail/fs/ctl .
.PP
If
.B /mail/fs
is not already being served and
.B -f
names a Unix mbox file,
.I nedmail
starts
.IR upas/fs (1)
on it first.
.B -m
selects the mailbox name to read (default
.BR mbox ).
.B -c
names the SMTP configuration file used by reply and forward (default
.BR /mail/lib/smtp );
see
.B Sending
below.
.PP
At the prompt, which shows the current message number, a line is
.IB address command " " args
where either part may be omitted: a bare
.I address
prints that message and a bare newline advances to the next one.
.SS Addresses
.TP
.I n
message number
.TP
.B .
the current message
.TP
.B $
the last message
.TP
.B ^
the first message
.TP
.BI + n
.I n
messages forward (default 1)
.TP
.BI - n
.I n
messages back
.TP
.BI / regexp /
the next message whose from, subject or to matches
.SS Commands
.TP
.B p
print the message (a short header block, then the decoded body)
.TP
.B P
print the raw message
.TP
.B "\(dq"
print the body quoted with a leading attribution
.TP
.B h
print a one-line summary of the message
.TP
.B b
print summaries of the next ten messages
.TP
.B H
print the message's MIME structure
.TP
.B d
mark the message for deletion
.TP
.B u
remove the deletion mark
.TP
.B =
print the message number
.TP
.BI w " file"
save the decoded body to
.I file
.TP
.BI s " file"
append the raw message to
.I file
(as an mbox)
.TP
.B y
.PD 0
.TP
.B i
resynchronise with the mailbox, picking up new mail
.PD
.TP
.B q
quit, deleting the marked messages from the mailbox
.TP
.B x
quit without deleting anything
.TP
.BR r ", " R " [" \fIaddr\fP ]
reply to the sender (and any extra
.IR addr );
.B R
also quotes the original.
The reply body is typed at the prompt and ended with a line containing only
.BR . .
.TP
.BR a ", " A " [" \fIaddr\fP ]
reply to the sender and all original recipients;
.B A
quotes the original.
.TP
.BR m ", " M " " \fIaddr\fP ...
forward the message to
.IR addr ;
.B M
first reads a covering note (ended with
.BR . ).
.TP
.B help
print a command summary
.SS Sending
Reply and forward compose a message and hand it to
.IR upas-send (1)
over TLS. The server and credentials come from the SMTP configuration file
.RB ( -c ,
default
.BR /mail/lib/smtp ),
one
.I "key value"
per line, keys
.BR server ", " ip ", " port ", " user ", " passfile " and " from .
With no configuration file the message is composed and shown but not sent.
.SH EXAMPLE
Read an mbox, delete the second message, and quit:
.EX

	% upas/nedmail -f /tmp/mbox
	3 messages
	1: 2
	...
	2: d
	2: q
	deleted 1 message

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/upas/nedmail.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR upas/fs (1),
.IR upas-fetch (1),
.IR upas-send (1)
.SH BUGS
The pipe and shell escapes
.RB ( \(ba ,
.BR \(ba\(ba ,
.BR ! )
and message filing
.RB ( f )
are not implemented: in the hosted
.I emu
a spawned filter that ends with the
.I exit
instruction takes the reader down with it, so piping a message to an
arbitrary command is unsafe there (it works on native Inferno). See
.BR doc/upas-plan.md .
Reply reads its body from the command stream, so it is not a full-screen
editor; there is no address-book alias expansion yet.
